oxo compounds (Probable). It is spontaneously inactivated by its substrate and reactivated by EutA (By similarity). May play a role in BMC assembly or maintenance (Probable)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29531136"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Expression of the eut operon allows this bacteria to use ethanolamine (EA) as a carbon, nitrogen and energy source. It relies on cobalamin (vitamin B12) both as a cofactor for the ethanolamine ammonia-lyase activity and to induce the operon. EA enhances bacterial survival in macrophages in a concentration-dependent manner, suggesting it is an important nutrient during infection (PubMed:29531136)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"ethanolamine = acetaldehyde + NH4(+)","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:15313"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15343"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:28938"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:57603"}],"ecNumber":"4.3.1.7","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"1550360"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"2656649"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}]}},{"commentType":"COFACTOR","cofactors":[{"name":"adenosylcob(III)alamin","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"1550360"}],"cofactorCrossReference":{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:18408"}}],"note":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"}],"value":"Binds between the large and small subunits."}]}},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Amine and polyamine degradation; ethanolamine degradation"}],"commentType":"PATHWAY"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"1550360"}],"value":"The basic unit is a heterodimer which dimerizes to form tetramers. The heterotetramers trimerize; 6 large subunits form a core ring with 6 small subunits projecting outwards"}],"commentType":"SUBUNIT"},{"commentType":"SUBCELLULAR LOCATION","note":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"23585538"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"22428024"}],"value":"Probably located in the BMC; as EutC is targeted to the BMC interior this subunit should be in the same location (Probable). Has been suggested to be on the exterior of the BMC (PubMed:23585538)"}]},"subcellularLocations":[{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"22428024"}],"value":"Bacterial microcompartment","id":"SL-0544"}}]},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Part of the 17-gene eut operon transcribed from a single promoter, induced by ethanolamine and adenosylcobalamin (AdoCbl, vitamin B12)"}],"commentType":"INDUCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10464203"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16585748"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"23585538"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"26565973"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"2656649"}],"value":"No aerobic growth on ethanolamine (EA) supplemented with cobalamin (vitamin B12) (PubMed:10464203, PubMed:2656649). No phenotype during growth in vitro. Decreased expression of eutR during bacterial growth in vitro. About 10-fold outcompeted by wild-type in mouse intestine at 2 and 4 days following oral infection, but no effect seen during growth in peritoneal exudate macrophages (PubMed:26565973). A non-polar deletion mutant does not grow on EA between pH 5.5 and pH 8.5, stops acetaldehyde release on EA plus vitamin B12 (PubMed:16585748). A deletion allows growth on acetate, suggesting BMC assembly or maintenance is impaired (PubMed:23585538)"}],"commentType":"DISRUPTION PHENOTYPE"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"}],"value":"Belongs to the EutB family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":453,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Ethanolamine ammonia-lyase large subunit","featureId":"PRO_0000087083"},{"type":"Binding site","location":{"start":{"value":160,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":162,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"}],"ligand":{"name":"substrate"}},{"type":"Binding site","location":{"start":{"value":193,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":193,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"}],"ligand":{"name":"substrate"}},{"type":"Binding site","location":{"start":{"value":194,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":194,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","featureCrossReferences":[{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:18408"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"}],"ligand":{"name":"adenosylcob(III)alamin","id":"ChEBI:CHEBI:18408"}},{"type":"Binding site","location":{"start":{"value":246,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":246,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","featureCrossReferences":[{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:18408"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"HAMAP-Rule","id":"MF_00861"}],"ligand":{"name":"adenosylcob(III)alamin","id":"ChEBI:CHEBI:18408"}},{"type":"Binding site","location